is defined as the smallest integer ℓ ∈ N such that every sequence S over G of length
|S| ≥ ℓ has k disjoint nonempty zero-sum subsequences. Recently, Gao et al. [13]
studied the connection between s≤k exp(G) (G) and sk exp(G) (G), i.e., the following
conjecture.
Conjecture 1.2. Let G be a finite abelian group. Then
                    sk exp(G) (G) = s≤k exp(G) (G) + k exp(G) − 1
for every k ∈ N.
   Up to now, the direct and inverse zero-sum problems of s≤k (G) have been widely
studied. Freeze and Schmid [8] obtained the exact number of s≤3 (C2r ), namely,
1 + 2r−1 . Lindström [28] studied the invariant s≤4 (C2r ) when r goes to +∞. Wang
et al. [33] determined s≤k (G) for G = Cm ⊕ Cn with m|n. Roy and Thangadurai
[29] considered the direct zero-sum problem of s≤k (G) for a finite abelian p-group
G with a large exponent by a different method from [33]. Recently, Zhang [34]
continued to study this problem for abelian groups of rank three.
   For the inverse zero-sum problem of s≤k (G), the known conclusions are less.
Davydov and Tombakis [6] studied the inverse zero-sum problem of s≤3 (C2r ) by the
method of coding theory. Recently, the inverse zero-sum problem of s≤k (G) with
r(G) = 2 has been also solved. For this, one can see [7, 21, 22, 23].
   Here, we only exhibit the result for the inverse zero-sum problem of s≤k (Cn ⊕Cn ).
