                                     P      group and S be a minimal zero-sum
sequence over G of length D(G). Then (S) = G.
Lemma 3.2 ([18]). Let G be a cyclic group of order n and S be a sequence over G
of length n. If S is minimal zero-sum, then S = g n , where g is a generator of G.
    By the above we can easily see that the following result holds.
Corollary 3.3. Let G be a cyclic group of order n and S be a sequence over G of
length ≥ n. If every zero-sum subsequence of S has the length ≥ n, then S = g |S| ,
where g is a generator of G.
   Let G be a finite abelian group and let A be a finite non-empty subset of G. The
stabilizer of A is defined by Stab(A) = {g ∈ G : g + A = A}.
Lemma 3.4 ([18]). Let G be a finite abelian group and let A and B be finite
non-empty subsets of G. If B ⊂ Stab(A), then ⟨B⟩ is a subgroup of Stab(A).
  Furthermore, the following statements are equivalent:
     (a) B ⊂ Stab(A).
     (b) A = A + B.
     (c) A = (g1 + ⟨B⟩) ∪ . . . ∪ (gr + ⟨B⟩) for some r ∈ N and g1 , . . . , gr ∈ A.
