Lemma 3.5. Let G be a finite abelian group with r(G) ≥ 2 and G does not have the
form C2 ⊕ C2m . Let S be a sequence over G of length D(G) + 1. If every zero-sum
subsequence of S has the length ≥ D(G) − 1, then the rank of ⟨S⟩ is at least 2.
Proof. Suppose that the rank of ⟨S⟩ is 1. Thus set ⟨S⟩ ∼ = Cn . Since r(G) ≥ 2, we
have that ⟨S⟩ is a proper subgroup of G, which implies that D(G) ≥ n+1. It follows
that every zero-sum subsequence of S has the length ≥ D(G) − 1 ≥ n. By Corollary
3.3 we have that S = g D(G)+1 , where g is a generator of ⟨S⟩. Obviously, S has a
zero-sum subsequence of length n. Hence, n ≥ D(G)−1 ≥ n, i.e., D(G) = n+ 1. By
a simple analysis, we must have that G ∼= C2 ⊕ Cn with 2|n, where Cn = ⟨S⟩ = ⟨g⟩.
This is a contradiction to our assumed condition.                                □
