codex
The full paper confirms the native object is a sequence in a finite abelian group and the witness is a nonempty index subsequence whose group sum is zero. It proves extremal existence bounds, not computational hardness. I’m testing the only defensible candidate: a native \(C_q^2\) modular subset-sum family at critical density, while explicitly checking the lattice attack that typically breaks low-density plantings. If that attack succeeds, I will reject the family.
